<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=typewriter">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Typewriter</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The typewriter is a mechanical or electromechanical machine that prints characters on paper when a key is pressed, a type element striking an inked ribbon against the page. Its invention was incremental: Rasmus Malling-Hansen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> invented the Hansen Writing Ball in 1865, and it went into commercial production in 1870 as the first typewriter sold. The first commercially successful model was patented in 1868 by Christopher Latham Sholes and others in Milwaukee, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>, and made by E. Remington and Sons from 1873 with the QWERTY layout, still the standard for English computer keyboards. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/personal-computer">Personal computers</a> largely replaced typewriters after the 1980s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/typewriter.jpg" alt="Typewriter" width="330" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: GodeNehler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MEK_II-371.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The typewriter was invented in small steps by many people working independently or in competition over several decades, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobile</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> were. Historians have estimated that some form of it was invented 52 times. The Hansen Writing Ball, invented by Rasmus Malling-Hansen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1865, went into commercial production in 1870 and was the first typewriter sold. It printed only capital letters and was still reported in use in European offices in 1909.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Each key of a typewriter causes one character to be printed on paper by striking an inked ribbon against the page with a type element. The first commercially successful machine was patented in 1868 in Milwaukee, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>, by Christopher Latham Sholes, Frank Haven Hall, Carlos Glidden and Samuel W. Soule. The clockmaker and machinist Matthias Schwalbach made the working prototype.</p><p>Sholes and James Densmore bought out the others and agreed with E. Remington and Sons, then known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sewing-machine">sewing machines</a>, to sell it as the Sholes and Glidden Type-Writer, which gave the machine its name. By about 1910 the manual typewriter had a fairly standard design of typebars striking a ribbon against a cylindrical platen.</p><p>Manufacturers at first found it hard to sell. Buyers compared a Remington costing 125 dollars with a pen costing a penny, and some recipients of typed letters felt insulted. Makers trained customers and referred typists to employers, and business colleges began to teach typing. Remington assumed the machine would be used for taking dictation and operated by a woman, and women filled many clerical posts during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Mark Twain bought a Remington in autumn 1874 and was using it for correspondence by December. The typewriter was also an early accessibility tool for blind people, and Sir Francis Campbell began adapting one with Braille output in 1875.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Sholes and Glidden machines established the QWERTY layout, which remains the standard for English-language keyboards, and variants such as the French AZERTY and German QWERTZ arose for other languages. Typewriters were a fixture of offices until the 1980s, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/personal-computer">personal computers</a> began to replace them, and IBM sold its typewriter division in 1991. Habits such as typing two hyphens for a dash, and terms such as backspace, carriage return and cc for carbon copy, descend from the machine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Typewriter” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mkhedruli is the script of everyday writing, and the standard Windows keyboard for it is essentially that of manual <b>typewriters</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/georgian">Georgian · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Slow and never sold commercially, it is often called the first American <b>typewriter</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-23">23 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1865</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Denmark</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Rasmus Malling-Hansen · Christopher Latham Sholes</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Historians estimate that some form of typewriter was invented 52 times: a British patent of 1714 granted to Henry Mill appears to describe a similar machine, and Pellegrino Turri built one for a blind friend between 1801 and 1808.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=typewriter">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
